\title{GeoSafe: Internalizing Collision Geometry into Flow-Matching VLA Weights for Zero-Overhead Safe Manipulation}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have advanced robot manipulation toward general-purpose deployment, yet collision safety remains an open practical challenge for real-world use. Most existing safety methods operate at inference time, adding computational overhead; training-time alternatives based on reinforcement learning reduce violations but lack geometric grounding for precise spatial collision reasoning.

We propose \ours{}, a training-time approach that embeds collision awareness directly into flow-matching VLA weights. During fine-tuning, a lightweight auxiliary head predicts per-timestep Signed Distance Field (SDF) values from the model's internal representations, and a collision-shaping loss penalizes trajectories that violate a safety margin. A noise-aware weighting function $w(t)$ suppresses this penalty at high noise levels where geometric predictions are unreliable, activating it only in the low-noise regime where action predictions carry meaningful spatial structure. After training, the SDF head is discarded entirely: the model runs through the standard inference pipeline with zero additional latency, zero additional memory, and no runtime access to scene geometry (SDF fields, meshes, or depth maps).

We evaluate \ours{} on the SafeLIBERO benchmark (32 collision scenarios across 4 task suites), measuring Task Success Rate (TSR) and Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR). \ours{} achieves \placeholder{XX.X}\% TSR and \placeholder{XX.X}\% CAR, compared to \placeholder{XX.X}\% TSR / \placeholder{XX.X}\% CAR for vanilla $\pi_{0.5}$ and \placeholder{XX.X}\% TSR / \placeholder{XX.X}\% CAR for a representative inference-time baseline. Our approach draws an analogy between guidance distillation in image generation and safety internalization in robot control, providing a ``train-with, infer-without'' framework for embedding geometric safety constraints into generative action policies.
\end{abstract}

%=============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
%=============================================================================

Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as a powerful paradigm for general-purpose robot manipulation. $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi0} demonstrated that a flow-matching architecture built on a pretrained vision-language model can master diverse manipulation tasks across multiple robot platforms, and its successor $\pi_{0.5}$ \citep{black2025pi05} extended this capability to open-world generalization through co-training on heterogeneous data. These models generate smooth, language-conditioned action trajectories through learned flow-matching.

Consider a robot clearing a cluttered shelf: reaching for a target mug, its arm trajectory passes within millimeters of nearby glasses---a collision invisible to the action policy. Its flow-matching velocity field encodes no notion of obstacle proximity. Yet a critical gap separates laboratory success from real-world deployment: collision safety. When a robot executes a manipulation task in a cluttered environment, unintended contact with surrounding objects or the workspace boundary can damage both the robot and the environment, and create safety hazards for nearby humans. The problem is especially acute for VLA models, which generate entire action chunks in a single forward pass without explicit geometric reasoning about obstacle proximity.

Existing approaches to VLA collision safety fall into two categories. Inference-time methods, including OmniGuide \citep{song2026omniguide} (SDF-guided denoising, ${\sim}2\times$ latency overhead), AEGIS/VLSA \citep{hu2025aegis} (CBF trajectory projection), SafeDiffuser \citep{xiao2025safediffuser} (CBF-embedded diffusion), add computational overhead and risk pushing corrected trajectories into out-of-distribution states where subsequent predictions degrade. Critically, these methods require runtime access to scene geometry (signed distance fields, meshes, or depth maps) and online constraint solving, limiting deployment to environments with accurate geometric models. Training-time methods such as SafeVLA \citep{zhang2025safevla} reduce violations through constrained RL but rely on abstract cost functions without geometric grounding. A fundamental tension persists: inference-time interventions add latency and risk OOD drift, while training-time RL methods lack geometric structure to reason about collision boundaries. Reinforcement learning with collision penalties offers a training-time path, but requires online simulator interaction and careful reward shaping; an auxiliary regression loss can instead be applied directly to offline demonstrations without modifying the base training objective.

We draw on an observation from image generation: guidance distillation \citep{meng2023distillation} has shown that inference-time guidance signals can be internalized into model weights during training. We transfer this principle to VLA safety, internalizing SDF collision geometry into flow-matching velocity fields for safe action generation. At low noise levels ($t$ near 0), the model's internal representations encode meaningful geometric structure; an SDF auxiliary head attached to these representations provides a differentiable collision proximity signal. By penalizing collision-prone predictions with a noise-dependent weight that activates only in the low-noise regime, we shape the learned velocity field to route around obstacles without corrupting the training signal at high noise levels.
